Bloom's floating tab bar and progressive blur
(src/tab-bar/, src/progressive-blur/)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Derived from expo-glass-tabs v0.1.1
  https://github.com/davidmokos/expo-glass-tabs
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 David Mokos

Bloom derives the interaction and motion design from expo-glass-tabs v0.1.1: the
minimize-on-scroll progress model, the sliding highlight with finger scrubbing,
the constant-driven capsule geometry, the ten-layer progressive-blur falloff, and
the fade-on-focus tab screen. Each derived module carries a provenance header
naming the upstream file and version it came from.

The following are re-implemented rather than ported:

  - Theming. Upstream hardcodes a dark palette (#FFFFFF tints, an
    rgba(10,10,12,0.55) surface, a black blur tail), which reads as a black bar
    in a light theme. Every color role is instead resolved from Bloom's design
    tokens through `resolveTabBarTheme`, so the bar follows the active
    `BloomThemeProvider` preset and mode, with an optional partial override.

  - Icons. Upstream's icon API is SF Symbols. Bloom's primary API is
    `item.icon`, any node — the same convention as `Tabs`/`TabsTrigger` and
    `FrostedIconButton` — so the bar renders identically on every platform.
    `item.sfSymbol` remains available as an iOS-only enhancement.

  - Platform forks. Upstream is one iOS-first module. Bloom splits the capsule
    surface and the glyph into neutral / `.native` / `.web` variants, and forks
    the progressive blur so the web build uses one masked `backdrop-filter`
    instead of ten stacked `BlurView`s. The native-only packages therefore never
    reach a web bundle or a consumer's type-check.

  - Router coupling. Upstream binds directly to expo-router. Bloom's core
    `@oxyhq/bloom/tab-bar` has no router dependency at all; the bindings live in
    a separate `@oxyhq/bloom/tab-bar/expo-router` subpath, so the bar is usable
    in an app with no router.

  - Reanimated dependency arrays. Every mapper declares the shared values it
    reads, which is what makes the animation run on web without the
    react-native-worklets babel plugin.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bloom's menu, popover, select, typography, chip, aspect-ratio and checkbox APIs
(src/dropdown-menu/, src/context-menu/, src/menubar/, src/popover/,
src/floating/, src/select/, src/typography/, src/chip/, src/aspect-ratio/,
src/checkbox/)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

API derived from react-native-reusables, revision 119d0b10 (2026-07-02)
  https://github.com/founded-labs/react-native-reusables
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Founded Labs

  Sources read: packages/registry/src/nativewind/components/ui/{dropdown-menu,
  context-menu,menubar,popover,select,text,badge,aspect-ratio,checkbox}.tsx.
  The registry package carries no version, so the git revision is cited instead.

What Bloom takes is the PUBLIC API and nothing under it: the component names,
the composition structure and the prop shapes, so a shadcn/react-native-
reusables call site ports across without being rewritten. No upstream code is
included, and every implementation below the API is Bloom's own — which is why
these modules carry no per-file provenance headers, unlike the two entries
above.

Deliberately NOT taken, each replaced by an existing Bloom system:

  - `@rn-primitives/*`. Every upstream family is a thin wrapper over one of
    these, and each brings its own portal, overlay and slot machinery. Bloom
    already has `portal/`, `overlay/OverlayRoot`, `overlay/stack.ts` and
    `overlay/dropdown-placement`; a second overlay system beside them is what
    decides which surface paints on top by accident.

  - `lucide-react-native`. Bloom ships `icons/`.

  - `TextClassContext` and upstream's `text.tsx`. Bloom's typography is
    `styled(RNText)` through react-native-css, and its `H1`-`H6` already carry
    `role="heading"` plus `aria-level`, which upstream's `h1`-`h4` variants do
    only on web.

  - `cn` and `class-variance-authority`. Bloom is not a Tailwind-first library;
    its variants resolve through `theme/accent-colors.ts`.

  - Hardcoded colours and hardcoded `z-*` classes. Every colour is a resolved
    Bloom token, and depth is decided once by open ORDER in `overlay/stack.ts`
    rather than by what a surface IS.

  - `react-native-screens`' `FullWindowOverlay`. Bloom's native surfaces present
    through `dialog/SheetShell`, which is already a React Native `<Modal>`
    window.

One deliberate behavioural divergence, documented at the component and in
`docs/dropdown-menu.mdx`: a sub-menu (`*MenuSub` / `*MenuSubTrigger` /
`*MenuSubContent`) renders as an INLINE DISCLOSURE on both platforms rather than
as a web flyout. The API is unchanged.

